-- Original, introductory BBA Y2S1 lessons for the MUST pilot.
-- These are Ace-authored learning guides, not lecturer-approved lecture notes or exam answers.
INSERT INTO public.ace_lessons
  (slug,title,subject_slug,subject_label,level,reading_minutes,summary,learning_outcomes,sections,review_questions,illustration,accent,published)
VALUES
('bba-hrm-foundations','Human resource management: from workforce planning to performance','human-resource-management','Human Resource Management','Intermediate',10,
 'Understand how an organisation plans, attracts, develops, supports and evaluates the people needed to deliver its objectives. Original Ace introduction for BFB3200; follow the current lecturer outline for examinable detail.',
 ARRAY['Explain the purpose and scope of human resource management','Distinguish workforce planning, recruitment and selection','Connect training, performance management and employee relations'],
 jsonb_build_array(
  jsonb_build_object('heading','HRM starts with organisational needs','body','Human resource management is the coordinated management of people at work. It links staffing decisions to an organisation’s strategy, structure, culture and legal obligations. The aim is not simply to fill vacancies: the organisation needs the right capabilities, in the right roles, at the right time, while treating employees fairly.','example','A Meru retailer planning a second branch estimates the roles, shift coverage, customer-service skills and supervision it will need before recruiting.'),
  jsonb_build_object('heading','Plan, recruit and select','body','Workforce planning compares the people and skills currently available with future requirements. Job analysis clarifies the tasks and competencies of a role; a job description explains duties and reporting lines, while a person specification describes the capabilities required. Recruitment attracts a pool of applicants. Selection assesses applicants against job-related criteria using structured interviews, tests, work samples and reference checks.','example','For a credit officer role, a structured scenario exercise can test customer communication and basic affordability reasoning more consistently than an unstructured conversation.'),
  jsonb_build_object('heading','Develop, reward and manage performance','body','Induction helps new employees understand the role, standards and organisation. Training addresses current skill gaps; development prepares people for future responsibilities. Performance management sets expectations, gives regular feedback and reviews evidence against agreed objectives. Compensation includes financial and non-financial rewards. Employee relations concerns communication, representation, workplace conduct, grievances, discipline and compliance with applicable employment law.','example','A sales team can agree clear service and quality targets, review progress monthly and identify coaching needs instead of relying only on end-of-year judgement.'),
  jsonb_build_object('heading','Measure whether HR practices work','body','Useful measures depend on the decision being made. Examples include time to fill a vacancy, staff turnover, absenteeism, training completion, internal promotion, employee relations cases and performance against role objectives. Metrics need context: a low turnover rate is not automatically good if poor performers are never addressed, and a high training attendance rate does not prove learning transfer.','example','Compare new-staff retention and customer complaint rates before and after an induction redesign, while checking whether the roles and reporting periods are comparable.')
 ),
 jsonb_build_array(
  jsonb_build_object('question','How does workforce planning differ from recruitment?','answer','Workforce planning identifies future people and skill requirements and compares them with current capacity. Recruitment is one possible response used to attract applicants when a staffing gap exists.'),
  jsonb_build_object('question','Why should selection criteria be linked to job analysis?','answer','Job analysis identifies actual duties and competencies. Linking selection to it makes decisions more relevant, consistent, explainable and defensible.'),
  jsonb_build_object('question','Name two ways an organisation can evaluate training effectiveness.','answer','It can assess demonstrated skill or knowledge before and after training and examine whether performance or work outcomes improve after employees return to the role.')
 ),'briefcase','sage',true),

('bba-business-law-contracts','Business law: agreements, contracts and remedies in Kenya','business-law','Business Law','Intermediate',12,
 'Build a foundation for analysing business agreements: identify the parties, offer, acceptance, consideration, intention, capacity, terms and possible remedies. Original Ace introduction for BFB3201; verify legal rules against current Kenyan legislation and cases.',
 ARRAY['Identify the elements commonly examined when analysing contract formation','Distinguish terms, representations and factors that may affect consent','Explain why breach and remedies require analysis of facts and applicable law'],
 jsonb_build_array(
  jsonb_build_object('heading','Start with the legal issue and the facts','body','Business law provides rules for commercial relationships, transactions and disputes. In a problem question, first identify the parties, the transaction, the issue and the relevant facts. Avoid jumping straight to a conclusion. State the legal principle, apply it to the facts and explain the likely outcome, noting any uncertainty or missing information.','example','If a supplier delivers fewer units than an order states, identify the agreed quantity, delivery terms, communications and any later variation before deciding whether there was a breach.'),
  jsonb_build_object('heading','How a contract may be formed','body','A contract analysis commonly examines offer, acceptance, consideration, intention to create legal relations and capacity, together with any required formalities. An offer is a sufficiently definite expression of willingness to contract on stated terms. Acceptance must correspond to the offer and be communicated in a legally effective way. Consideration concerns the value exchanged, subject to applicable legal rules and exceptions. Commercial intention is often inferred from the context, but it should not be assumed without examining the facts.','example','A written quotation may be an offer or an invitation to treat depending on its wording and context. A customer response that changes quantity or price may be a counter-offer rather than acceptance.'),
  jsonb_build_object('heading','Terms, consent and breach','body','Contract terms define the parties’ obligations. Distinguish express terms from terms implied by law or fact, and distinguish contractual terms from representations that induced a party to enter the agreement. Misrepresentation, mistake, duress or undue influence may affect consent and legal consequences. A breach occurs when a party fails to perform an obligation when due, subject to the contract and applicable law.','example','If a business buys equipment based on a specific capacity claim, preserve the advertisement, quotation, messages, inspection records and contract because the exact statement and reliance may matter.'),
  jsonb_build_object('heading','Remedies and Kenyan legal context','body','Remedies depend on the nature of the breach, the contract, evidence, statutory rules and judicial principles. Damages may compensate for legally recognised loss; other remedies can include specific performance, injunctions, rescission or restitution where the requirements are met. Do not assume every breach produces every remedy. For Kenyan business-law questions, consult current Kenyan statutes, amendments and decisions from authoritative sources such as Kenya Law.','example','A buyer seeking compensation should identify the contractual obligation, the breach, the loss claimed, causation and evidence supporting the amount.')
 ),
 jsonb_build_array(
  jsonb_build_object('question','Why is an advertisement not automatically a contractual offer?','answer','Its wording and context matter. It may invite customers to make offers rather than show a definite intention to be bound to anyone who responds.'),
  jsonb_build_object('question','What is the purpose of consideration in a contract analysis?','answer','It examines the value exchanged between the parties, subject to applicable legal rules and exceptions.'),
  jsonb_build_object('question','What is a sound structure for answering a business-law problem question?','answer','Identify the issue, state the relevant legal principle, apply the principle to the specific facts, and give a reasoned conclusion with any necessary qualification.')
 ),'scales','indigo',true),

('bba-entrepreneurship-opportunity','Entrepreneurship: test an opportunity before investing','entrepreneurship','Entrepreneurship','Intermediate',10,
 'Move from an idea to an evidence-based opportunity using customer discovery, feasibility, business models and early experiments. Original Ace introduction for BFB3202; use the lecturer’s prescribed frameworks and local examples.',
 ARRAY['Distinguish a business idea from a tested opportunity','Use customer discovery to investigate a problem','Assess market, operational and financial feasibility','Design a small experiment before committing significant resources'],
 jsonb_build_array(
  jsonb_build_object('heading','An idea is a hypothesis','body','An idea becomes a stronger opportunity when a real customer group has a meaningful problem, existing alternatives are inadequate, and a viable solution can be delivered sustainably. Entrepreneurs operate under uncertainty. They reduce it by testing assumptions about the customer, problem, willingness to pay, delivery and costs rather than treating enthusiasm as evidence.','example','A student considering a campus laundry service should first learn how students currently wash clothes, what frustrates them, how often they would pay and which alternatives already serve the campus.'),
  jsonb_build_object('heading','Customer discovery and value proposition','body','Customer discovery uses interviews, observation and small tests to understand behaviour. Ask about recent real experiences rather than only asking whether someone likes an idea. A value proposition explains which customer problem is addressed, what outcome is offered and why the solution is preferable to alternatives. Segmentation helps focus on a reachable group with similar needs.','example','Interview students who paid for laundry in the past month. Ask what they paid, how long it took, what went wrong and how they chose the provider.'),
  jsonb_build_object('heading','Feasibility and the business model','body','Market feasibility considers customers, demand, competition and access to the market. Technical and operational feasibility considers skills, suppliers, equipment, processes and capacity. Financial feasibility examines start-up costs, pricing, contribution, cash timing and the volume needed to cover costs. A business model explains how value is created, delivered and captured; it is broader than a slogan or a business-plan document.','example','A delivery service may have customer demand but still fail if rider availability, delivery distance, fuel, failed deliveries and customer acquisition costs make each order unprofitable.'),
  jsonb_build_object('heading','Run a small, measurable experiment','body','A pilot should test the riskiest assumption at the lowest sensible cost. Define the target customer, the offer, the test period, the success measure and a decision rule before starting. Track actual behaviour such as paid orders, repeat use, conversion and contribution margin. Use results to improve, change direction or stop.','example','Offer a limited number of pre-orders to one customer segment and measure paid conversion and fulfilment cost before buying stock or renting premises.')
 ),
 jsonb_build_array(
  jsonb_build_object('question','What is the difference between a business idea and an opportunity?','answer','An idea is a proposed solution. An opportunity has evidence of a meaningful customer problem, a reachable market and a plausible way to create and capture value.'),
  jsonb_build_object('question','Why are questions about recent customer behaviour stronger than asking if someone likes an idea?','answer','Recent behaviour provides evidence of actual needs, choices and spending. Positive opinions about a hypothetical idea may not translate into adoption or payment.'),
  jsonb_build_object('question','What should a useful pilot measure?','answer','It should measure the assumption being tested using observable behaviour such as paid conversion, repeat use, delivery time, customer acquisition cost or contribution margin.')
 ),'briefcase','amber',true),

('bba-leadership-practice','Leadership: influence, decisions and team performance','leadership','Leadership','Intermediate',9,
 'Understand leadership as influence toward shared goals, distinguish it from management, and examine how context, ethics, motivation and communication shape team performance. Original Ace introduction for BFB3203.',
 ARRAY['Distinguish leadership from management while recognising their overlap','Compare trait, behavioural and situational approaches','Explain how motivation, communication and ethics affect teams'],
 jsonb_build_array(
  jsonb_build_object('heading','Leadership and management work together','body','Leadership concerns influencing people toward a shared direction. Management coordinates resources, processes, responsibilities and control. The roles overlap: a manager may need to lead change, and a leader may need to organise delivery. Strong organisations need direction and execution rather than treating the two as competing labels.','example','A branch manager sets a clear service improvement goal, explains why it matters, listens to staff concerns and also allocates shifts, tracks complaints and reviews results.'),
  jsonb_build_object('heading','Different approaches fit different situations','body','Trait approaches examine characteristics associated with leadership, but no single trait guarantees effectiveness. Behavioural approaches examine what leaders do, such as task direction, support, participation and communication. Situational and contingency approaches emphasise the relationship between leadership behaviour, follower readiness, task uncertainty and context. Transformational leadership focuses on purpose, change and development; transactional leadership clarifies expectations and exchanges.','example','A new team handling a safety-critical process may need clear instructions and close feedback. An experienced team solving a novel customer problem may benefit from more participation and autonomy.'),
  jsonb_build_object('heading','Motivation, ethics and team climate','body','Motivation is shaped by needs, expectations, fairness, goals, recognition and the design of work. Leaders influence the conditions in which people contribute. Ethical leadership requires consistency, accountability, fair treatment, responsible use of authority and willingness to address misconduct. Psychological safety does not mean avoiding standards; it means people can raise concerns and admit mistakes without humiliation.','example','A supervisor who invites staff to report process errors, investigates fairly and follows through on improvements is more likely to surface risks than one who punishes every person who speaks up.'),
  jsonb_build_object('heading','Evaluate leadership by outcomes and conduct','body','Leadership effectiveness should be considered against task outcomes, team capability, trust, staff development and ethical conduct. Short-term results alone may hide burnout, turnover or poor decisions. Use evidence from more than one source and consider the time horizon.','example','A sales leader should examine customer suitability and complaint trends alongside revenue, not reward volume while ignoring harmful selling practices.')
 ),
 jsonb_build_array(
  jsonb_build_object('question','Why is leadership not identical to management?','answer','Leadership emphasises influence and direction, while management emphasises coordination and execution. In practice, effective managers and leaders often need both.'),
  jsonb_build_object('question','What is the central idea of a situational leadership approach?','answer','The most effective behaviour depends on the task, context and needs or capability of the people involved; no single style is best in every situation.'),
  jsonb_build_object('question','Why should a leader evaluate more than short-term output?','answer','Short-term output may conceal burnout, turnover, poor conduct, low trust or capability gaps that damage long-term organisational performance.')
 ),'brain','sage',true),

('bba-public-relations-trust','Public relations: build trust with planned communication','public-relations','Public Relations','Intermediate',10,
 'Learn how organisations research publics, set communication objectives, choose channels, manage relationships and evaluate outcomes. Original Ace introduction for BFB3205; distinguish strategic public relations from simply posting on social media.',
 ARRAY['Define public relations as a planned relationship-management function','Identify publics and set measurable communication objectives','Choose channels and messages that fit the situation','Evaluate communication using outcomes rather than exposure alone'],
 jsonb_build_array(
  jsonb_build_object('heading','Public relations is relationship work','body','Public relations is a planned communication and relationship-management function between an organisation and the groups that affect or are affected by it. These groups are often called publics or stakeholders. PR is not limited to publicity, press releases or social media. It includes listening, explaining decisions, responding to concerns, building understanding and maintaining credibility over time.','example','A university-facing business may need different communication for students, suppliers, staff, regulators and the local community because each group has different interests and information needs.'),
  jsonb_build_object('heading','Research before choosing a message','body','A communication plan begins by defining the issue, researching the organisation’s context and identifying relevant publics. Establish what each group knows, believes, needs and can do. Set a measurable objective, such as improving understanding of a process or increasing attendance at a specific event. A message should be accurate, consistent, accessible and appropriate to the audience.','example','Before announcing a service change, identify who uses the service, what disruption they may face, what questions they will ask and where they expect updates.'),
  jsonb_build_object('heading','Choose channels and manage the response','body','Channel choice depends on audience access, urgency, complexity, trust and the need for dialogue. A notice may communicate a simple update; a briefing or meeting may be better for complex change. Media relations require accuracy and responsiveness. Crisis communication should prioritise verified facts, affected people, clear actions and timely updates. Do not speculate or conceal material facts.','example','If a delivery delay affects customers, give the known facts, explain what is being done, state when the next update will arrive and provide a contact route for individual cases.'),
  jsonb_build_object('heading','Evaluate outcomes and protect ethics','body','Outputs include activities such as releases issued, events held or posts published. Outtakes include whether people noticed, understood or remembered the message. Outcomes include changes in knowledge, attitudes, trust or behaviour. Evaluation should match the objective and use more than vanity measures. Ethical PR requires honesty, disclosure, respect for privacy and correction of inaccurate information.','example','For a customer education campaign, measure whether customers can correctly explain the new process and complete it successfully, not only how many views a post received.')
 ),
 jsonb_build_array(
  jsonb_build_object('question','How is public relations different from publicity?','answer','Publicity is one communication output. Public relations is a broader planned function that researches publics, manages relationships, communicates and evaluates outcomes.'),
  jsonb_build_object('question','What is the difference between an output and an outcome in PR evaluation?','answer','An output is an activity or item produced, such as a press release. An outcome is a resulting change in understanding, attitude, trust or behaviour.'),
  jsonb_build_object('question','What should a crisis update include when facts are still emerging?','answer','It should state verified facts, acknowledge affected people, explain immediate actions, identify what is not yet known and give a clear time or channel for the next update.')
 ),'market','slate',true),

('bba-cost-accounting-decisions','Cost accounting: classify costs and support decisions','cost-accounting','Cost Accounting','Intermediate',12,
 'Build a practical foundation for cost classification, costing methods, cost behaviour, contribution and break-even analysis. Original Ace introduction for BFC3227; follow the lecturer's prescribed methods and formats.',
 ARRAY['Classify costs by traceability, function and behaviour','Distinguish product and period costs','Explain job and process costing at an introductory level','Calculate contribution and break-even output using stated assumptions'],
 jsonb_build_array(
  jsonb_build_object('heading','Why managers need cost information','body','Cost accounting measures and analyses the resources used to produce goods or deliver services. It supports pricing, budgeting, cost control, inventory valuation and operational decisions. A cost is meaningful only in relation to a purpose and a cost object, such as a product, service, department, customer or project.','example','A bakery may calculate the cost of a cake order separately from the monthly cost of rent, because the first helps estimate order contribution while the second is a period overhead.'),
  jsonb_build_object('heading','Classify costs carefully','body','Direct costs can be traced economically to a cost object; indirect costs require allocation. Manufacturing or service costs may be classified by function. Fixed costs remain constant in total within a relevant activity range and period, while variable costs change in total with activity. Mixed costs contain fixed and variable elements. Product costs are attached to inventory or service production where applicable; period costs are expensed in the period incurred.','example','Flour used in a specific batch is usually a direct material cost. Factory rent is usually indirect and fixed within the relevant range. Electricity may contain both fixed and variable elements.'),
  jsonb_build_object('heading','Choose a costing method','body','Job costing accumulates costs for separately identifiable jobs or orders. Process costing accumulates costs for large volumes of similar units moving through continuous or sequential processes. The method should reflect how work is produced. Overhead allocation uses a selected cost driver or allocation base; the choice should be reasonable and understood because it affects reported unit costs.','example','A custom furniture workshop may use job costing for each client order, while a producer making identical bottled drinks may use process costing.'),
  jsonb_build_object('heading','Use contribution and break-even analysis','body','Contribution per unit is selling price per unit less variable cost per unit. Under the simple cost-volume-profit model, break-even units equal total fixed costs divided by contribution per unit. The calculation assumes a relevant range, stable selling price and variable cost per unit, and a defined sales mix where multiple products exist. It is a planning model, not a guarantee of actual results.','example','If fixed costs are KSh 60,000 and contribution is KSh 300 per unit, break-even output is 200 units (60,000 ÷ 300), assuming the model's conditions hold.'),
  jsonb_build_object('heading','Interpret the result before acting','body','Cost information depends on assumptions, allocation choices and data quality. A manager should state the period, activity level, cost driver and relevant range, then distinguish relevant future costs from sunk costs when evaluating alternatives. Do not assume that an allocated accounting cost is avoidable simply because a product or department is discontinued.','example','A special order may be worthwhile when its incremental revenue exceeds relevant incremental costs and it does not displace more valuable work, even if the quoted price is below a fully allocated average cost.')
 ),
 jsonb_build_array(
  jsonb_build_object('question','What is the difference between a fixed cost and a variable cost?','answer','Within a relevant range and period, a fixed cost stays constant in total as activity changes, while a variable cost changes in total with the activity level.'),
  jsonb_build_object('question','When is job costing more suitable than process costing?','answer','Job costing is suitable when work can be identified and costed by individual order or job. Process costing is more suitable for large volumes of similar output through continuous or sequential processes.'),
  jsonb_build_object('question','If fixed costs are KSh 60,000 and contribution is KSh 300 per unit, what is break-even output?','answer','200 units, calculated as fixed costs divided by contribution per unit (60,000 ÷ 300), assuming the simple cost-volume-profit assumptions hold.')
 ),'calculator','amber',true)
